informal:
Let $\{P_\theta : \theta \in \Theta\}$ be a model, $\Theta_0$ the null set and $\Theta_1$ the alternative set. A randomized test $\varphi$ is uniformly most powerful (UMP) at level $\alpha$ when

1. $\varphi$ is a critical function, i.e. measurable with values in $[0,1]$;
2. it has level $\alpha$: $\beta_\varphi(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_\theta \varphi \le \alpha$ for every $\theta \in \Theta_0$;
3. for every competing critical function $\psi$ of level $\alpha$ and every $\theta \in \Theta_1$ one has $\beta_\psi(\theta) \le \beta_\varphi(\theta)$.

The word *uniformly* refers to clause 3 holding simultaneously at every alternative: a single test beats every competitor everywhere on $\Theta_1$. When $\Theta_0$ and $\Theta_1$ are singletons this is the notion of a most powerful test of a simple hypothesis against a simple alternative that the Neyman–Pearson lemma solves.

